---
/** Pillar page: every retail share class ranked after federal and state tax, then the explanation. */
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import Comparator from '../../components/calc/Comparator';
import Sources from '../../components/Sources.astro';
import { organizationSchema, websiteSchema, webApplicationSchema, webPageSchema, faqSchema, type FAQItem } from '../../lib/seo';
import { FUNDS, META, TBILLS, fund, pct, bn, usd, day, drag, monthName, avgYield, rank, KIND_LABEL, KIND_LEGEND, YIELD_DAYS } from '../../lib/data';
import { fiftyTest } from '../../lib/engine/tax';
import { ROWS, METHOD, href, PAGES } from '../../lib/common';
import { GROUPS } from '../../i18n/nav';
import { title as fitTitle, desc as fitDesc } from '../../lib/snippet';

const asOf = day(META.month);
const month = monthName(META.month.slice(0, 7));
const n = FUNDS.length;
const total = FUNDS.reduce((s, f) => s + f.assets, 0);
const kinds = ['treasury-only', 'treasury', 'government', 'prime', 'muni', 'state-muni'] as const;
const byKind = kinds.map((k) => ({ k, ...avgYield((f) => f.k === k) }));
// Same set as the California page and the 50% guide (test naïf 2026-11-08: « 92 of 197 » here, « 92 of 221 » there).
const govs = FUNDS.filter((f) => f.k === 'government' || f.k === 'treasury' || f.k === 'treasury-only');
const fails = govs.filter((f) => fiftyTest(f.q) === 'fails');
const govYield = byKind.find((x) => x.k === 'government')!.mean;
const muniYield = byKind.find((x) => x.k === 'muni')!.mean;
const drags = FUNDS.map((f) => drag(f)).filter((x): x is number => x != null).sort((a, b) => a - b);
const ca = rank({ amount: 50000, state: 'CA', filing: 'single', income: 120000 });
const tx = rank({ amount: 50000, state: 'TX', filing: 'single', income: 120000 });
const topBefore = [...FUNDS].sort((a, b) => b.y - a.y)[0];
const caPosOfTop = ca.findIndex((x) => x.f.t === topBefore.t) + 1;
const vmf = fund('VMFXX'), vus = fund('VUSXX'), swv = fund('SWVXX'), sns = fund('SNSXX');
const caOf = (t: string) => ca.find((x) => x.f.t === t)!.r;
const txOf = (t: string) => tx.find((x) => x.f.t === t)!.r;
/** Gap in points between two yields as shown (rounded to 0.01 first), so the reader can check it against the figures. */
const gapPts = (a: number, b: number) => ((Math.round(a * 10000) - Math.round(b * 10000)) / 100).toFixed(2);

const title = fitTitle(['Money Market Fund Yields After Tax, by State, as Filed', 'Money Market Fund Yields After Tax by State, as Filed']);
const description = fitDesc([
  `Money market fund yields for ${n} retail share classes as filed with the SEC for ${asOf}, ranked by what they leave after federal and state income tax.`,
  `Money market fund yields of ${n} retail share classes as filed with the SEC for ${asOf}, ranked by what they leave you after federal and state income tax.`,
]);
const faqs: FAQItem[] = [
  { q: 'Which money market fund has the highest yield after tax?', a: `It depends on your state and bracket, which is why the table above asks for them. For a single Californian with $120,000 of taxable income, the top fund on the filings for ${month} was ${ca[0].f.t} at ${pct(ca[0].r.afterTaxYield)} after tax; in Texas it was ${tx[0].f.t} at ${pct(tx[0].r.afterTaxYield)}. Past yields, not a forecast.` },
  { q: 'Are money market fund dividends taxed by states?', a: `Yes, except the part earned on U.S. Treasury securities and on debt of agencies such as the Federal Home Loan Banks, which federal law shields from state tax. California, Connecticut and New York exempt that part only when the fund held at least 50% of its assets in such obligations at each quarter end.` },
  { q: 'Why is the yield here different from the fund company’s site?', a: `This site shows the 7-day net yield each fund reported to the Securities and Exchange Commission in its monthly Form N-MFP filing, for the last business day of ${month}. Fund companies publish a new figure every business day. Using the filed figure puts every fund on the same date.` },
  { q: 'How often is this page updated?', a: `For now the update is run by hand: once funds have filed a new month of Form N-MFP reports (they have five business days after month end), the data is downloaded and the site rebuilt. There is no automatic update, so the figures age between two updates. The current data comes from reports filed from ${day(META.filedFrom)} to ${day(META.filedTo)}.` },
  { q: 'Is a money market fund safe?', a: `A money market fund is not a bank deposit and is not insured by the FDIC. Government and Treasury funds hold short U.S. government debt and repo; prime funds also lend to banks and companies. Rule 2a-7 limits their maturity and requires daily and weekly liquid assets, but losses remain possible.` },
  { q: 'Is this site giving investment advice?', a: `No. Money Fund Yields is an independent site, not an investment adviser, and it sells no funds. It ranks past yields as filed and applies published tax rates to the situation you type. The result is an estimate, not tax or investment advice; check a fund’s prospectus and tax letter before acting.` },
  { q: 'Do municipal money funds beat taxable ones?', a: `Sometimes, in high brackets. National municipal funds averaged ${pct(muniYield)} on the filed data, free of federal tax, against ${pct(govYield)} for government funds. The municipal fund keeps more only when tax takes more than ${pct(1 - muniYield / govYield, 0)} of a taxable dividend, federal and state combined. Single-state funds add a state exemption for residents of that state only.` },
];
const jsonLd = [organizationSchema('en'), websiteSchema('en'), webApplicationSchema({ name: 'Money market fund after-tax yield comparator', description, path: '/en/', lang: 'en' }), webPageSchema({ name: title, description, path: '/en/', lang: 'en' }), faqSchema(faqs, 'en')];
---
<PageLayout title={title} description={description} lang="en" jsonLd={jsonLd} faqs={faqs} showAuthor={true}>
  <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold text-navy-900 md:text-4xl">Money market fund yields after tax: which keeps the most?</h1>
  <p data-chrome class="mb-3 mt-2 text-navy-700">It depends on your state: for a single Californian at $120,000 of taxable income, <strong>{ca[0].f.t}</strong> keeps the most, {pct(ca[0].r.afterTaxYield)} after tax. The table ranks {n} versions (share classes) of {META.series} funds by what they leave <em>you</em>.</p>
  <nav data-chrome aria-label="Where to start" class="mb-3 flex flex-wrap gap-2 text-sm">
    <a href="#find-fund" class="rounded-full border border-accent-300 bg-accent-50 px-3 py-1.5 font-semibold text-accent-800 hover:bg-accent-100">I hold a fund (SPAXX…) →</a>
    <a href="/en/money-market-vs-high-yield-savings/" class="rounded-full border border-accent-300 bg-accent-50 px-3 py-1.5 font-semibold text-accent-800 hover:bg-accent-100">My cash is in a bank →</a>
    <a href="#rank" class="rounded-full border border-navy-200 bg-white px-3 py-1.5 font-semibold text-navy-800 hover:bg-navy-50">Not sure? Start here →</a>
  </nav>
  <p data-chrome class="mb-3 text-xs text-navy-700"><strong>Not a bank account:</strong> not FDIC-insured, can lose money. Yields as filed with the SEC for {YIELD_DAYS}. Not investment advice.</p>
  <div id="rank" class="scroll-mt-20"><Comparator client:load rows={ROWS} asOf={month} methodHref={METHOD} /></div>

  <section data-chrome class="mt-8 rounded-xl border border-navy-200 bg-navy-50 p-4 text-navy-800 sm:p-5">
    <h2 class="text-lg font-bold text-navy-900">In short</h2>
    <ul class="mt-2 list-disc space-y-1 pl-5">
      <li>Ranked by what you keep after federal and state income tax, not by the yield before tax: two funds paying the same can leave you different amounts, because your state taxes some funds and not others.</li>
      <li>Yields come from the funds’ {month} reports, so they are a few weeks old; the fund’s own site shows today’s figure.</li>
      <li><strong>Not a bank account:</strong> a money market fund is not insured by the FDIC; a bank savings account is insured up to $250,000 per depositor, per bank.</li>
    </ul>
    <details class="mt-3"><summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-navy-900">New to money market funds? What one is and what protects it</summary>
    <p class="mt-2">A money market fund is a mutual fund that lends money for short periods, from a day to a few months, mostly to the U.S. government and, for some funds, to banks and companies. It aims to keep each share at $1.00 and pays you the interest as dividends. <strong>It is not a bank account and is not insured by the FDIC.</strong> What protects it instead: under the SEC’s rule 2a-7, a fund may hold only short, high-quality debt, with an average maturity of 60 days at most, and must keep at least a quarter of its money in cash or assets it can turn into cash within one business day. A government fund keeps at least 99.5% in cash, U.S. government securities and loans backed by them. These limits are not a guarantee: a fund can still lose money. <a class="underline" href="/en/money-market-fund-rules/">The rules in detail</a>.</p>
    <p class="mt-2">Seeing SPAXX or VMFXX in your brokerage account? That is the fund where Fidelity or Vanguard keeps your uninvested cash: <a class="font-semibold text-accent-700 underline" href="/en/spaxx/">SPAXX</a> · <a class="font-semibold text-accent-700 underline" href="/en/vmfxx/">VMFXX</a> · <a class="font-semibold text-accent-700 underline" href="/en/spaxx-vs-vmfxx/">SPAXX vs VMFXX</a>.</p>
    <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-navy-700">This site does not say where a fund can be bought. Funds are usually bought through a brokerage account, and each brokerage chooses which funds it offers, with what minimum and what fee: check yours before moving cash.</p>
    </details>
  </section>
  <section data-chrome class="mt-10">
    <h2 class="mb-3 text-xl font-bold text-navy-900">Pages on this site</h2>
    {[...GROUPS].sort((a, b) => (a.id === 'compare' ? -1 : b.id === 'compare' ? 1 : 0)).map((g) => { const ps = PAGES.filter((p) => p.group === g.id); return <details class="mb-3 rounded-lg border border-navy-200 px-3 py-2" open={g.id === 'compare'}><summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-navy-900">{g.label} <span class="text-sm font-normal text-navy-600">· {ps.length} pages: {ps.slice(0, 3).map((p) => p.nav).join(', ')}…</span></summary><ul class="mt-3 grid gap-2 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3">
      {ps.map((p) => <li><a href={href(p.id)} class="block rounded-lg border border-navy-200 px-4 py-3 hover:border-accent-300 hover:bg-accent-50"><span class="font-semibold text-accent-700">{p.nav}</span><span class="block text-sm text-navy-600">{p.card}</span></a></li>)}
    </ul></details>; })}
  </section>

  <details class="mt-8 rounded-xl border border-navy-200 p-4 sm:p-5">
    <summary class="cursor-pointer text-lg font-bold text-navy-900">How the ranking works, in detail: tax by state, the 50% rule, averages, data</summary>
  <div class="prose prose-slate mt-10 max-w-none">
    <h2>What this table shows that a yield list does not</h2>
    <p>Most lists of money market fund yields stop at the 7-day yield, the number fund companies put at the top of their pages. It is the right number to start with and the wrong one to finish with, because two funds with the same yield can leave very different amounts after tax. A Treasury fund’s dividend escapes state income tax; a prime fund’s does not; a government fund sits in between, and in California, Connecticut and New York it can fall on either side of a line drawn at 50% of its assets. The table above takes the yield each fund reported to the Securities and Exchange Commission, the holdings it reported on the same form, and the federal and state tax rates that apply to your income, and ranks the {n} retail share classes by what they leave you. Change the state and the order changes. On the filings for {month}, the fund with the highest yield before tax, {topBefore.t} at {pct(topBefore.y)}, ranked {caPosOfTop} for a Californian with $120,000 of taxable income.</p>

    <h2>Why the yield is not what you keep</h2>
    <p>A money market fund pays its income as dividends, and those dividends are taxed as ordinary income at your federal rate, plus the 3.8% net investment income tax above $200,000 of income for a single filer and $250,000 for a joint return. That part is the same for every taxable fund. The state part is where funds differ. Federal law forbids states to tax interest on obligations of the United States, and the same protection covers the debt of a few agencies created by Congress, among them the Federal Home Loan Banks, the Farm Credit Banks and the Tennessee Valley Authority. It does not cover Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac, and it does not cover repurchase agreements, even when the collateral is Treasuries, because the interest is paid by the bank or dealer that borrowed, not by the government.</p>
    <p>So the share of a dividend that a state cannot tax depends on what the fund held. Vanguard Federal (VMFXX), Vanguard’s largest money market fund, held {pct(vmf.usob, 0)} of its portfolio in exempt obligations at the end of {month}; Vanguard Treasury (VUSXX) held {pct(vus.usob, 0)}; Schwab Prime Advantage (SWVXX), a prime fund, held {pct(swv.usob, 0)}. For a single Californian at $120,000, those differences turn VMFXX’s {pct(vmf.y)} into {pct(caOf('VMFXX').afterTaxYield)} after tax, VUSXX’s {pct(vus.y)} into {pct(caOf('VUSXX').afterTaxYield)}, and SWVXX’s {pct(swv.y)} into {pct(caOf('SWVXX').afterTaxYield)}. In Texas, with no state income tax, the same three give {pct(txOf('VMFXX').afterTaxYield)}, {pct(txOf('VUSXX').afterTaxYield)} and {pct(txOf('SWVXX').afterTaxYield)}{txOf('SWVXX').afterTaxYield > txOf('VUSXX').afterTaxYield ? ': with no state tax to avoid, the prime fund comes out ahead.' : `: the prime fund is still behind the Treasury fund, but by ${gapPts(txOf('VUSXX').afterTaxYield, txOf('SWVXX').afterTaxYield)} point instead of ${gapPts(caOf('VUSXX').afterTaxYield, caOf('SWVXX').afterTaxYield)} in California, because there is no state tax to avoid.`}</p>

    <h2>The 50% line: {fails.length} of {govs.length} government and Treasury classes miss it</h2>
    <p>California, Connecticut and New York add a condition. A fund’s dividends from U.S. obligations are exempt in those states only if, at the close of each quarter of the fund’s tax year, at least half of its assets were in such obligations. Miss the line at one quarter end and the whole dividend for the year is taxable in those states, not just the part from repo or agency paper. The holdings in the Form N-MFP reports let anyone check the line. At the June 30 and September 30 quarter ends in the data, {fails.length} of the {govs.length} retail government and Treasury share classes here held less than 50% in Treasuries and exempt agency debt at least once. Some carry the word Treasury in their name: Schwab Treasury Obligations (SNOXX) held most of its money in Treasury repo and stayed below the line at both quarter ends. The <a href="/en/fifty-percent-rule/">50% rule guide</a> explains the test and shows how to read it from a filing.</p>
    <p>Other states take a simpler route. New Jersey, Pennsylvania and Illinois exempt the share of dividends earned on federal obligations, whatever the fund’s mix, and New Jersey does so even for funds that miss its separate 80% rule for qualified investment funds. Nine states have no tax on interest and dividends at all. The comparator applies the rule of the state you choose; for states whose rules this site has not read, it asks for your rate and assumes the federal share is exempt, and says so.</p>
  </div>

  <section data-chrome class="prose prose-slate mt-8 max-w-none">
    <h2>Average 7-day yield by type of fund, {month}</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th class="text-right">Share classes</th><th class="text-right">Average</th><th class="text-right">Lowest</th><th class="text-right">Highest</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{byKind.map((x) => <tr><td>{KIND_LABEL[x.k]}</td><td class="tabular-nums text-right">{x.n}</td><td class="tabular-nums text-right">{pct(x.mean)}</td><td class="tabular-nums text-right">{pct(x.min)}</td><td class="tabular-nums text-right">{pct(x.max)}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
    </table></div>
    <p>{KIND_LEGEND}</p>
    <p>Simple averages of the 7-day net yields filed for the last business day of the month, one row per share class, not weighted by assets. For comparison, the Treasury’s 4-week bill rate on {day(TBILLS.date)} was {pct(TBILLS.w4)} and the 13-week rate {pct(TBILLS.w13)}, on a coupon-equivalent basis.</p>
  </section>

  <div class="prose prose-slate mt-8 max-w-none">
    <h2>What the averages hide</h2>
    <p>The spread inside each type is wider than the gap between types. Government funds ranged from {pct(byKind.find((x) => x.k === 'government')!.min)} to {pct(byKind.find((x) => x.k === 'government')!.max)}, and most of that range is cost: the filings give each fund’s gross yield and each class’s net yield for the same day, so the difference is what the class pays in expenses. The cheapest classes give up a few hundredths of a point; {drags.filter((x) => x > 0.005).length} classes give up more than half a point, and the most expensive {pct(drags[drags.length - 1])}, about {usd(drags[drags.length - 1] * 100000)} a year on $100,000. Prime funds earn a little more on their holdings because they lend to banks and companies, but after expenses their average sits close to the government average, and their dividends carry almost no state exemption. Municipal funds yield less on paper, about {pct(muniYield)} on average, because their dividends are free of federal tax; their yields also move more from week to week than any other type.</p>
    <p>Each fund page on this site shows the daily filed yield since {day(META.firstYieldDate)}, the holdings on the last report, the quarter-end test, the expense drag and the fund’s rank after tax in three states. The comparison pages put two funds, or a fund and a savings account, side by side in one calculator.</p>

    <h2>Where the numbers come from</h2>
    <p>Every money market fund registered in the United States files a Form N-MFP report each month with the Securities and Exchange Commission, within five business days after month end. The report lists every security the fund held, the fund’s weighted average maturity and life, its assets by share class, and the 7-day net yield of each class for every business day of the month. The Commission publishes these reports as filed and also releases them as monthly data sets. This site reads both: the data sets for June to September and the individual reports filed from {day(META.filedFrom)} to {day(META.filedTo)} for {month}, {META.classes} share classes and {META.series} funds in all after leaving out classes with a minimum above $100,000, institutional prime and tax-exempt classes, and classes under $50 million.</p>
    <p>Two consequences follow. The yields here are not today’s: they are the last business day of the reported month, and by the time the next report is filed they are about five weeks old. And they are as filed: if a fund later amends a report, the change appears at the next update. For now the figures are downloaded and the site rebuilt by hand; there is no live feed and no automatic update. The <a href={METHOD}>data and method page</a> describes each step, the classification of holdings, and where the estimate from holdings and a fund’s year-end tax letter can differ.</p>

    <h2>How the after-tax yield is worked out</h2>
    <p>For each share class the comparator takes the filed 7-day yield and your amount to get a year of dividends at that yield. It applies your federal marginal rate from the 2026 brackets of IRS Revenue Procedure 2025-32, adds the net investment income tax when your income is above the threshold, and then the state rate from your state’s 2025 schedule. The state part is applied only to the share of the dividend that is not exempt: the holdings estimate of U.S. obligations, or the full dividend if your state applies the 50% test and the fund missed it. Municipal funds skip the federal tax, and single-state municipal funds skip the state tax in their own state. The result is a rate, not a promise: yields change every day and your actual tax depends on your whole return.</p>

    <h2>Money funds, savings accounts and Treasury bills</h2>
    <p>A high-yield savings account is a bank deposit, insured by the FDIC, and its interest is taxed by your state in full. A Treasury bill bought directly pays interest that no state can tax, and you hold it to a fixed date. A money market fund sits between: its share price is held at $1.00, it can be sold any business day, and part of its dividend may escape state tax. The <a href="/en/money-market-vs-high-yield-savings/">savings comparison</a> and the <a href="/en/treasury-bills-vs-money-market-funds/">Treasury bill comparison</a> run the numbers for your state.</p>
  </div>
  </details>

  <details class="mt-8 rounded-xl border border-navy-200 p-4" data-chrome><summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-navy-900">Sources and data credits</summary><div class="prose prose-slate mt-2 max-w-none">
    <Sources lang="en" intro="Fund yields and holdings come from SEC filings, and every tax rule and rate from the IRS, state tax agency or statute listed here, each read on" />
    <p>Fund data: Securities and Exchange Commission, Form N-MFP, as filed; public information. This site is not affiliated with the Commission or with any fund company. Fund names and tickers belong to their owners and are used only to identify the funds. Total assets of the share classes in the table: {bn(total)}.</p>
  </div></details>
</PageLayout>
